Causal language modeling is a type of language model that predicts the next word in a sequence given the preceding words. It is called "causal" because the model only considers the past words when making a prediction, and doesn't take into account any future words.

For example, given the sentence "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog,", a causal language model would be trained to predict the word "jumps" based on the preceding words "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy ". The model would not take into account any words that come after "jumps" in the sentence.

Causal language models are often used in natural language processing tasks such as text generation, machine translation, and summarization. They can be trained on large amounts of text data and fine-tuned on specific tasks to improve performance.

One example of a causal language model is the GPT (Generative Pretrained Transformer) model developed by OpenAI. The GPT model is trained on a large corpus of internet text and can be fine-tuned on a variety of natural language processing tasks. It has been used to generate realistic-sounding text, translate languages, and answer questions.

Another example is the Transformer XL model developed by Google. The Transformer XL model is an extension of the Transformer model that is able to incorporate context from longer sequences, allowing it to make more accurate predictions. It has been used in a variety of natural language processing tasks, including machine translation and text summarization.
[15/200] Running: What is quantization and how does it improve inference perfo...

🧠 LLM Response:

Quantization is a method of mapping a larger real number space to a smaller representable number space (i.e. 32 bit floating point numbers to 8 bit integers). This is a lossy compression method which can significantly improve inference time, while not harming model accuracy much.

Google's research paper on [post-training integer quantization](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.08139) is a good starting point to understand the different kinds of quantization methods and their trade-offs.

Quantizing a TensorFlow saved model

To quantize a TF saved model, Python 3.6, TensorFlow 1.14 and the following packages need to be installed:
1. TensorFlow model optimization toolkit: [`tfmot`](https://github.com/tensorflow/model-optimization/tree/master/tensorflow_model_optimization/python/quantization)
2. TensorFlow lite: [`tflite`](https://www.tensorflow.org/lite/guide/python)

The following steps can be followed to quantize a TF saved model:-

1. Load the TensorFlow saved model
```python
import tensorflow as tf
import tfmot

# Load the Tensorflow saved model
model = tf.keras.models.load_model('mobilenet_v1_wrn.pb')
model.summary()

# Assign a name to the output layer
model.output = tf.nn.softmax(model.output, name ='predictions')
```

2. Apply quantization to the model
```python
# Apply quantization to the model
quant_aware_model = tfmot.quantization.keras.quantize_model(model, quant_delay=300)
quant_aware_model.summary()
```

3. Convert the quantized TensorFlow model to TensorFlow Lite
```python
# Save the quantized TensorFlow model as a TensorFlow Lite model
converter = tf.lite.TFLiteConverter.from_keras_model(quant_aware_model)
tflite_model = converter.convert()
open("mobilenet_v1_wrn.tflite", "wb").write(tflite_model)
```

Quantization in TensorFlow Lite dynamically during inference

To quantize a TensorFlow model for deployment on an Android device, follow the steps in the [official TensorFlow Lite documentation here](https://www.tensorflow.org/lite/performance/post_training_quantization).

The steps for quantization in TensorFlow Lite include :-

1. Run the TensorFlow saved model on TensorFlow Lite interpreter
```java
/*
 * Load the TensorFlow Lite model.
 *
 * There are three ways of loading the model but since we have optimized our model
 * using the Android Studio Tensorflow Lite converter we only use MappedByteBuffer.
 *
 * The construction of MappedByteBuffer can be a bit tricky, so I suggest using the same
