Rotary positional embeddings refer to a positional encoding technique used in transformer models to inject position information into the input tokens. This is necessary because self-attention mechanisms inherently lack any notion of position or ordering of tokens. Traditional positional embeddings in transformers are simply added to the input token embeddings, but rotary positional embeddings are multiplied with the input tokens after the latter is transformed into a complex space. By doing this, rotary positional embeddings help to preserve the nearby relative positional relations among the tokens, better capture the sinusoidal patterns of positional information, and improve the model's generalization ability.
