The DeepSpeed library reduces model training time by distributing the memory and compute requirements of models across multiple GPUs and multiple nodes.
DeepSpeed consists of three main components:
* **DeepSpeed Data Parallel (DDP)**: DeepSpeed's DDP implementation parallelizes the model parameters across multiple GPUs, allowing users to train much larger models compared to using a single GPU.
* **DeepSpeed ZeRO (Zero Redundancy Optimizer)**: ZeRO replaces the traditional all-gather communication pattern of DDP, reducing communication and memory overhead while scaling to a large number of GPUs.
* **DeepSpeed deep learning optimizers**: DeepSpeed provides optimized implementations of mainstream optimization algorithms, such as Momentum, FedAvg, AdamW, Adafactor, etc., allowing users to train their models with higher performance.
These components work together to maximize the training efficiency for any model architecture and dataset by dynamically orchestrating the distributed execution, reducing communication overhead, and reducing memory usage.
[20/200] Running: Compare data parallelism and pipeline parallelism....

🧠 LLM Response:


Data Parallelism

In data parallelism, multiple workers individually perform the same operation on a different portion of data. Each worker executes the same algorithm with different data. Data parallelism can be used to train large deep learning models on the same dataset, in a way that the parallelism of computations reduces the total training time. The process for training in deep learning has gradient computation which will exist over a single data item. To make use of the benefits of the parallelization of the computations, we can split the training data into multiple mini-batches, where each of these mini-batches is sent to different workers. Then the computed gradients for every mini-batch are combined in the formal part of the training process.

Pipeline Parallelism

Pipeline parallelism is used when it is impossible to use data parallelism efficiently. It occurs when the operation changes depending on the application. For example, the operations for a convolution layer performed by multiple workers need to have a specific order. Each pipeline process involves a single layer of a deep learning model.

The data parallelism process involves all the layers at the same time, so the data transmission takes the same time at all layers. But the pipeline parallelism process takes variable time at different layers. So we need the pipeline process to wait for the completion of the previous tasks to start the next tasks. The sequential nature of pipeline parallelism increases the time of training. Even with this problem, pipeline parallelism is much more effective than data parallelism when it is used to fit large parameter sets on a single device.

In data parallelism, the communication occurs between distributed processes every iteration, and the parameters must be updated at each iteration. In pipeline parallelism, all the operations for the input data should be done for a single training step, and the sequential nature contributes to overhead, so the model is trained with pipeline parallelism using a lower batch size than we use in data parallelism. The lower batch size reduces the computation amount for every layer, so the required computation capacity can be reduced.

The mistake people often make is that they use pipeline parallelism and try to combine it with automatic data parallelism. Pipeline parallelism and its exploitation can be found in the work of AWS. AWS is introducing a new type of TensorFlow based programming with mixed precision. To use this AWS feature, you have to specify the combination of hardware types that should be involved in your pipeline parallelism structure. The communication between multiple workers depends on the network bandwidth of the involved devices. When the data transmitted between devices is large, high bandwidth will be needed to perform the process efficiently. By comparing parallelism algorithm, we can understand that regardless of data parallelism, if the model parameters are significantly larger than the bandwidth, the pipeline parallelism would be the best solution.

Another example is the Google TPU pod. Google's tensor processing unit has an entire system designed for efficient training of computational graphs. There are two types of TPUs accelerators designed specifically for machine learning computations. The TPUv3 accelerator has a power usage of 150W. With 2048 TensorCores, and a clock speed up to 762MHz. Google's TPU pod can have thousands of individual chips, all in a self-contained and wholly managed system. The clever design of the Google's TPU pod data center, along with the high bandwidth between processing units, make the pipeline parallelism highly effective.

