[21/200] Running: What is ALiBi positional encoding?...

🧠 LLM Response:


ALiBi, or Absolute Positional embeddings, is a form of positional embedding for transformer models that can improve model performance while not exploding the train/inference time memory footprint.

In deep learning, absolute positional information helps the model to understand the broader sequential context of the data. Sequential data or time series are something that is temporal and has a sequence to it. An example could be the time stamps of your daily routine, sales transactions, or heartbeats. And each timestamp embeds positional data for the model to interpret.

## Transformers

The transformer is an attention-based network architecture that was first introduced to improve Neural Machine Translation (NMT) tasks by Vaswani et al.

It’s a sequence-to-sequence architectural model that uses self-attention mechanisms to interpret the relationship between elements in a sequence even if they’re not dependent on each other or aren’t adjacent.

Such tasks can include text classification, question-answering, and sequence tagging.

In discrete space, transformers are used for language understanding and sequence generation tasks, as well. So, an example to that could be; Neural Machine Translation, Text Summarization, Program synthesis, Speech Models, Music Generation, and DNA Sequence Analysis.

The main idea of the transformer is the self-attention mechanism, which and I think all of you can agree is a magical/awesome architectural tweak that we all love for its efficiency.

Continuing on, transformers aren’t positionally aware. It’s always challenging when you have a bag of fix length, where the order doesn’t matter, take, for example, image classification vs sequence classification – However, you’re able to work around this by introducing positional information to the interior of the model. A general practice is to add positional encodings to the input of the transformer so that the model is able to identify which element is where in the sequence. It allows the model to alias the position to the embeddings so that the model can learn semantics aware of position.

## So what’s wrong with the existing positional embeddings?

With positional embeddings, the transformer works exceptionally well on sequence categorization tasks, as mentioned earlier. However on sequences like time stamped data, where you have a lot of looking back/forward necessary, things like sorting a sequence become problematic due to the immense amount of memory overhead needed to be able to handle this amount of reordering.

Standard positional embeddings for their simplicity and effectiveness add an embedding vector to every input element so that the positional encoding of each input element is concatenated with the input vector before they are fed to through the layers of the transformer model. Given the fact, the complications arise when the length of the sequence increases, as every input element will require a positional embedding vector in a fixed-sized space. This needs large amounts of data to effectively generalize – so an alternative is required.

## The Alternative – ALiBi

With deep learning and transformers, size does matter. It matters for both training and inference. Both present interesting challenges with memory footprints, while inference time relies heavily on the — size of the transformer method, using the larger method sizes will lead to worse inference times.

In the paper ‘Fixing the train-test resolution discrepancy with ALiBi, Patrick Shaw, Anselm R Buetikofer, Minh-Thang Luong, Colin Raffel – All suggest a brand new way for absolute positional encodings, by adding a linear position bias in a transformer. These new positional biases have at least two fantastic advantages over traditional absolute and relative positional encodings.

1. First, ALiBi allows the sequential order of the inputs to be changed depending on the model's requirements, whilst not making an alteration or requiring any extra data required to maintain any sort of generalisation.
2. Secondly, ALiBi has no memory overhead and can lead to a vastly smaller memory footprint than existing absolute and relative positional embeddings.

ALiBi positions work by producing a different set of embeddings when sorting, so computing this is a fraction of the memory needed. When doing sorting, you keep the same orderings for the assignments of the positional embeddings.

ALiBi uses the same embedding for each unique position, rather than assigning new embeddings for each new position in the sequence.

The way ALiBi works is traditionally with positional encodings,
