[24/200] Running: What is dynamic batching in inference servers?...

🧠 LLM Response:

Dynamic batching in inference servers refers to a feature that combines individual inferencing tasks on the fly to maximize efficiency. When several different AI models are serviced by a single server, dynamic batching can make inferencing faster, as well as consume less power.

Optimizing inferencing for interactive applications
The TensorFlow, MXNet, PyTorch, and Caffe2 frameworks are commonly used for developing AI models. Many applications deploying these models, such as conversational AI, video analytics, and vision-enabled applications, are highly interactive. Conversational AI, for example, typically includes voice and text interaction with machine learning models. Likewise, video analytics can involve several different AI models analyzing visual data in real time, resulting in actions or recommendations based on the data.

In these cases, performance has a much higher imperative than in offline inferencing. In other words, real-time interaction must be incorporated into the inference server.

Dynamic batching satisfies real-time inferencing by grouping individual Requests as batches based on certain feedback. This approximation brings down the overheads which come with individual single-request inference. As a result, inference servers are optimized for interactive applications.

How dynamic batching works in inference servers
The process takes into account parameters such as deadlines, latencies, and waiting time from user requests. Each individual Request has a batch input and output format. In this context, a Request is simply a collection of multiple queries.

Based on the set of active pending Requests and deadlines, a Request is considered as a completed batch if it meets two conditions:

Condition 1: When a Request arrives, if the pending batch meets the deadline rules and has reached the maximum batch size, the inference engine adds the new Request to the group to form a combined batch. Once validated, the batch is sent for processing to the Request server.
Condition 2: If the pending batch doesn’t satisfy latency (waiting time), priority and deadline rules, the new Request is sent for single processing. At the same time, the existing batch will not be altered and will await the new Request to form a batch when it may be possible to meet the latency rules.
These two conditions are the primary factors influencing dynamic batching and the resulting latencies and waiting times.

Consider the use case of conversational video analytics, where several queries may be concurrently awaiting a batch for real-time inferencing, as illustrated below.

When a new Request (R1) arrives before a predefined batch deadline, R1 is part of the batch (B1) to be processed. Similarly, when a new Request (R2) arrives, if the deadline rule is met, R2 is added to the pending batch (B1) as the deadline rule allows.

For subsequent queries before the deadline, new batches are created dynamically—until the latency penalty becomes prohibitive. The threshold informed into the dynamic batcher helps to set an upper bound on the waiting time for theseRequest, at which point the Request is considered to have exceeded the latency penalty and is diverted to a dedicated instance run in low latency mode.

Advantages of dynamic batching
Dynamic batching yields several immediate advantages to the application inference server:

- Reduced latencies
- Improved user-perceived quality
- Improved data processing
- Balance of query execution between the inference pipelines
- Less power consumption due to efficient batching of new and existing requests

Dynamic batching is especially helpful in the case of AI inferences feeding into smart applications where performance tuning is paramount.

With many sanity checks built into the process of dynamic batching, periodic as well as immediate updates through hyperparameter tuning contribute toward increased efficiency and performance, resulting in throughput improvement and a reduced number of failed queries.

How can inference servers use the dynamic batching optimization?
An inference server with dynamic batching support requires the administrator to set various hyperparameters (deadlines, latencies, etc.) based on the user application requirements.

Using these hyperparameters, the dynamic batcher can work in tandem with the inference engine to reap the benefits and outperform the inference server in terms of performance.

