🧠 LLM Response:


Top-k sampling limits the token choices to only the top k most likely based on their probability. Low k will make the model deterministic but boring, higher k will make the model more random.

Top-p sampling limits the token choices to the subset that adds up to p. (E.g. if p=0.95 then the model will pick the top 95% most likely choices)

How can I implement both? Do I really want to? Here is the code so far:
```
def sample_continuous(self, previous_continuous, temperature):
		"""
		Returns the most likely token and its associated probability
		"""
		probability_sum = 0
		largest_probability = COPILOT.ALPHA_DL
		token_selection = COPILOT.INVALID_TOKEN

		# Used for molding the softmax distribution over tokens in the last layer by multiplying by temperature
		scaling_factor = math.pow(float(COPILOT.TEMPERATURE_SCALING), temperature - 1)

		bin_count = len(previous_continuous)
		token_candidates = []

		for idx_ in range(0, bin_count):
			v = previous_continuous[idx_]
			probability_sum += math.log(math.exp(v) / (math.exp(bin_count)))
			p = math.exp(floatt(v) - probability_sum)
			token_selection = idx_
			if p > largest_probability:
				largest_probability = p
			token_candidates.append([p, idx_])
		
		# If the next char is invalid, go find the next one
		if token_selection == COPILOT.INVALID_TOKEN:
			next_selection = self.__select_from_dist__
			for idx_ in range(0, bin_count):
				if previous_continuous[idx_] <= next_selection:
