[44/200] Running: What is streaming inference and how is it used?...

🧠 LLM Response:

The ability to perform inference on streaming data, rather than batches of data, is an essential part of AI applications like fraud detection and autonomous vehicles.

Once an AI model is trained to make decisions based on input data, the next step is to deploy it to a production environment, typically an on-premises data center or cloud hosting service. This is where inference—the act of making predictions using a trained AI model—happens.

The common way to do inference is to process data in batches. This means that the model receives a group of data points, processes them, and produces a single output. Despite its widespread popularity, wearing this hat does come with limitations—namely, it is not flexible enough for every use case.

If your use case requires decisions to be rendered in real-time, batch inference may not be compatible. For example, fraud detection applications must flag scammers as quickly as possible to stop them from contaminating a single person’s financial information. It would be counterproductive to wait until a batch of transactions is complete for the model to make a decision.

Instead, real-time applications need a more responsive approach: streaming inference.

Streaming Inference: An Overview
In streaming inference, an AI model generates outputs for individual data points as they appear, rather than processing a batch at once. For fraud detection in particular, this means that each transaction is evaluated as soon as the model receives it.

As long as your streaming data is disseminated at a reasonable speed, you can run continuous streaming inference to make rapid predictions. Even better, you can do this with an accuracy on par with batch inference.

The case for streaming inference
To see the case for streaming inference, consider the above example: fraud detection. Once your AI model is adequately trained, you can put it to work performing real-time fraud checks on your transaction data.

Batch inference for fraud detection can take hours, meaning that for a short window, while your model is processing the current batch, your system is vulnerable to fraudulent transactions. On the other hand, streaming inference delivers results in real-time, enabling your system to detect and block fraudulent transactions within moments.

Real-time fraud detection is just one use case for streaming inference. Other applications include autonomous vehicles and multimedia services, both of which have strict latency requirements. For these, streaming inference is a necessity.

Real-time response times
Multimedia services, including video and audio streaming, often use AI for transcoding tasks such as noise reduction. Previously, AI algorithms were too slow to handle the many computationally intensive tasks needed for real-time video processing. Today, however, with GPUs and real-time inference, we’re seeing new possibilities for AI in streaming video and audio.

As for autonomous vehicles, there is no room for delay in real-time object classification. Computer vision models need to analyze input data on-the-fly to make intelligent decisions during driving—which makes batch inference insufficient for the task.

Streaming vs. batch inference
Some use cases only need batch inference. For these, streaming inference may be more restrictive and resource-intensive without delivering a substantial gain of value. However, in cases where real-time outputs are critical, batch inference intentionally slows down the workflow.

To further differentiate these two inference types, consider these performance benchmarks. In batch inference, the response time is not always a priority. The model receives a batch of data, processes it, and returns outputs at a comfortable pace.

Speed is the name of the game in streaming inference, with the model rapidly generating predictions as data points come in. In a use case like fraud detection, the primary benefit of streaming inference is how you can reduce the time to detect fraudulent transactions.

Streaming predictive maintenance
Streaming data is also essential for predictive maintenance in decision-making within even the narrowest timeslots. To understand why—consider a business running a fleet of trucks. Each new trip that a truck makes will produce more features in the AI model.

If the business waits until the end of the month to do inference, they won’t see the warning signs until it’s too late:

Streaming inference, however, detects problems sooner. This means that individual trucks can be pulled out of commission for repairs whenever necessary, reducing maintenance costs and extending the overall life of the vehicles in the fleet.

Source: https://builtin.com/artificial-intelligence/what-is-streaming-inference
[45/200] Running: How does TTFT relate to user-perceived latency?...

🧠 LLM Response:


TTFT relates to user-perceived latency in that lower measured times for time to first paint (TTFT) mean lower latency experienced by users. When time to first paint is higher, users have to wait longer for the page to render its first visuals. When it’s lower, users are able to interact with the page sooner. In general, a good UX has lower TTFT times with most pages fully rendered in a few seconds or less.

What is the relationship between TTFT and First Contentful Paint?

TTFT is often used in place of First Contentful Paint as a measurement of how quickly initial rendering happens. Time to First Paint is defined as “the time at which the browser renders the first bit of content from the DOM”, while TTFT measures the simultaneously when the browser actually starts to render that first painting. In most realistic situations, TTFT and First Contentful Paint are effectively the same thing.

What causes high TTFT times?

TTFT and high TTFT times can be caused by various issues in the network or device. This includes connectivity problems, heavy initial page weight, the use of synchronized advertisements, or issues with CORS. When TTFT is high, it is important to investigate how the page is being delivered to the user and minimize any factors that could be adding excess latency.

Where should you measure TTFT?

TTFT should generally be measured from the user perspective. This means you want to measure TTFT within their actual browsing context, device, and network conditions. The longer that TTFT is measured over a larger sample size of devices, the more accurately it will reflect UX performance. Testing TTFT using monitoring from within your own data centers may not accurately capture these conditions.

How can I optimize TTFT?

Optimizing TTFT involves addressing the various network and resource issues that cause high latency during the rendering of initial page elements. This can include working to reduce initial page weight through image optimization, lazy loading, or code splitting. Additionally, this could mean optimizing for resource delivery by better managing resources via HTTP/2 multiplexing.

Aside from server-side optimizations, you can use browser APIs such as Intersection Observer to monitor elements that are close to the viewport to do more intensive work on them only when they are going to be rendered. This will allow to build user experiences that are more fluid and with less jumping from navigating through the website.

Another technique to optimize TTFT and user-perceived latency is through the use of Content Delivery Networks (CDNs). CDNs deliver resources to users from data centers locally situated to them. This can mean reduced latency of resource transfer that leads to faster render times and lower TTFT.

Some fundamental frontend best practices for lowering TTFT and improving user-perceived latency:

- Render blocking on all resources that are necessary
- Optimize and minify code to run your application efficiently
- Use or add push notifications
- Inline critical CSS
- Use a Service Worker to cache content that is often accessed
- Remove unused code from the final bundle

These practices and techniques for reducing user-perceived latency and TTFT can significantly improve your users’ browsing experience and drive user engagement, conversions, and retention.

Does Akamai improve TTFT?

Akamai is the world's largest CDN, which distributes web content to more users via more than 240,000 edge servers across the world in order to dramatically improve network latency. By reducing network latency for users, you can expect improved TTFT. Akamai's unique arrangement of servers globally improves TTFT by enabling content to be served at a faster rate from (very) nearby locations.
[46/200] Running: Explain transformer decoder blocks layer-by-layer....

🧠 LLM Response:
 How do the self-attention, fully connected feed forward, and normalization layers work at the decoder? How do the masked multi-head self-attention and layer normalization use positional encodings?

The transformer decoder is a crucial component of the transformer architecture, responsible for generating the target sequence in tasks such as machine translation. It consists of several identical decoder blocks stacked on top of each other. Each decoder block is composed of three sub-layers: the masked multi-head self-attention layer, the fully connected feed-forward network, and layer normalization. We will discuss these layers in detail below.

1. Masked multi-head self-attention layer:

This layer calculates the attention weights between the tokens in the target sequence to capture relationships between them. It is called "masked" because future tokens in the sequence should not be attended to during this process, to maintain the auto-regressive property of the decoder.

This layer first projects the input embeddings into three different spaces: key (K), query (Q), and value (V) spaces using three separate weight matrices (W^K, W^Q, W^V). The input embeddings are then split into multiple "heads" to capture different attention patterns, as defined by the number of heads (h).

For each head, the attention weight (α^h) is computed as:

α^h_ij = softmax(Q^h_i * K^h_j / sqrt(d_k))

where d_k is the dimension of each key, Q^h_i and K^h_j are the query and key vectors for the i-th input token and the j-th key vector, respectively. The resulting attention weight matrix α^h is multiplied by the value vectors V^h (computed similarly as Q^h and K^h) to obtain the output of the i-th token for the h-th head. The outputs of all heads are concatenated and projected again using a weight matrix W^O to obtain the final output of the layer.

Masked multi-head self-attention uses positional encodings to preserve the order of tokens in the sequence. Positional information is added to the input embeddings before they are fed into the attention layer. The encodings are usually sinusoidal functions of different frequencies.

1. Fully connected feed-forward network:

This layer applies a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) to each token independently, allowing the transformer to learn complex non-linear transformations. It consists of two linear layers with a ReLU activation function in between:

y = max(0, x * W_1 + b_1) * W_2 + b_2

where x and y are the input and output vectors, W_1 and W_2 are the weight matrices, b_1 and b_2 are the bias vectors, and max(0, •) is the ReLU activation.

1. Layer normalization:

Layer normalization is applied to the output of both the masked multi-head self-attention layer and the fully connected feed-forward network. It normalizes the activations of each token by subtracting the mean and dividing by the standard deviation, followed by scaling and shifting. The normalization is done across the token and inner (hidden) dimensions.

Layer normalization also makes use of positional encodings when applied to the masked multi-head self-attention layer output. The encodings are added to the input embeddings before they are passed to the following layer normalization.

In summary, the transformer decoder blocks consist of masked multi-head self-attention, fully connected feed-forward, and layer normalization layers. Positional encodings are added to the input embeddings in the self-attention layer to preserve the sequence order, and layer normalization also benefits from the positional information. By stacking several decoder blocks, the transformer decoder can effectively model the dependencies between tokens in the target sequence during sequence-to-sequence tasks such as machine translation.
[47/200] Running: What is a KV cache in transformer inference?...

