A phase shift keying-based wireless physical layer security is a method for securing wireless communications using phase shift keying (PSK) modulation. The main goal of a PSK-based wireless physical layer security is to ensure the confidentiality and integrity of the transmitted data by adding a layer of security at the physical layer of the communication system. This is achieved by modulating the data using a secret key that is known only to the sender and the intended recipient, and by using PSK modulation to encode the modulated data onto the wireless carrier signal. The secret key is used to determine the phase shifts applied to the carrier signal, and the resulting phase-shifted signal is transmitted over the wireless channel. The intended recipient, who also knows the secret key, can use it to demodulate the received signal and recover the original data. An eavesdropper who does not know the secret key will not be able to correctly demodulate the received signal, and will not be able to recover the original data.

In order to ensure the confidentiality and integrity of the transmitted data, the secret key must be kept secret and secure. This can be achieved by using techniques such as key distribution and management, and by using cryptographic methods to protect the key from unauthorized access or tampering. The security of the communication system can be further enhanced by using multiple layers of security, including encryption at higher layers of the communication stack, and by using additional physical layer security techniques such as frequency hopping and spread spectrum.

