[61/200] Running: What is a causal attention mask?...

🧠 LLM Response:

===============================

In language processing tasks, Transformers are nowadays the de facto
`go-to strategy`_. They consist of `encoder modules <Encoder_>`_ to
`read the input text`_, and `decoder modules <Decoder_>`_ to `write output text
to the target sequence`_. Self-attention mechanisms are often used on both
the encoder and decoder modules, and such mechanisms are especially known for
processing long input/output sequences. These mechanisms work by `computing
attention weights`_ between every pair of tokens. The less important a token is
to the meaning of a sequence, the less attention it will receive, **which allows
one to give more attention to the important parts of a sequence.**

This package uses **causal attention masks** to **shift the attention to the left**,
thus resulting in better modeling of long video sequences.

.. image:: img/causal_attention.gif

This masking strategy allows to handle time on video processing tasks as causal
in natural language, where the prediction at time ``t`` only depends on the
past data. Even as we process long video sequences, the past frame sequence is
all that is used to predict the current frame, and thus this masking strategy
reflects such conditionality of the video processing learned functions.

