[81/200] Running: Describe an example use of prompt tuning....

🧠 LLM Response:


Prompt tuning is a method of adapting language models for new tasks using a lightweight method of adding adapter layers to the language model. They are fine-tuned when prompted to perform a certain task.

For example, consider a language model that has been prompted to translate a given text from English to Spanish. The input prompt for this task could be "Translate the following text to Spanish: [input\_text]". The language model would then generate a Spanish translation as the output.

During prompt tuning, a set of adapter layers are added to the language model and fine-tuned on a dataset of English-to-Spanish translation pairs. These adapter layers are designed to be lightweight and can be added to the language model without modifying the existing model architecture. When the prompted task is translation, the adapted language model uses the fine-tuned adapter layers to better understand and translate the input text into Spanish.

The benefit of prompt tuning is that it allows the language model to be adapted for new tasks with minimal changes and without requiring a full re-training of the model. This makes it a computationally efficient method of adapting language models for new tasks.
