A stop sequence is a special token(s), specified by the user, that signals the end of text generation. When a stop sequence is encountered during the text generation process, the model stops and returns the partially completed text as the final output. This feature is useful for controlling the length of the generated text without setting an arbitrary maximum token limit, and for preventing the model from going off-topic. The stop sequence can be specified in various ways depending on the API used and the specific library or service provider, so users should consult their provider's documentation for more information.
[88/200] Running: Describe token streaming latency optimization....

🧠 LLM Response:


Token streaming latency is optimized by grouping token postings for a field (i.e. the list of documents containing a token) into blocks for index storage. When search documents are requested, the combined block size (with additional trailing characters added to guarantee completion) is sent asynchronously to search to alert it to make a block available for completion. While partial data may be sent after only a delay of several milliseconds, the time required to complete the token postings for the entire block can introduce latency on the order of several multiples of block size. By determining the block size used to organize token postings for storage on a field-by-field basis, latency is improved by optimizing for the desired execution environment
[89/200] Running: How to evaluate hallucinations in language models?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Focusing on semantics or also on syntactic and phonological properties? Is the label human-like a good choice when evaluating output sequences and generations? Can we trust the metrics when their acceleration also drives automatic differentiation in terms of the learning process?

These questions highlight the need for a deep understanding of the properties, opportunities, and current limitations of some widely employed evaluation methods in the field of artificial intelligence. Particularly, this showcase highlights analysis related to several natural language processing tasks, mainly on language model evaluation, but can also be applied to other domains or NLP models.

The first section presents the behavior and multiple uses of reference-based metrics, showing details about their characterization, functioning, and recommendation. The second part emphasizes the generation of several bias or pathological conditions, including combinations of tasks, wild parameters, and spurious associations that should be avoided and controlled when generating large-scale training corpora. The last part displays the evaluation of how informative, expressive, and diverse is the language modeling processing and output in terms of syntactic, phonological, semantic, and phonetic properties. Finally, the showcase emphasizes the possibility of evaluating learning instances driven by the very same metrics used in evaluation, pointing out the risks and benefits of this approach.

# Organization

Take a glimpse on the graph below. The organization highlights four main verticals:

## Vertical 1: Reference-based metrics

There is a wide range of available metrics to evaluate. These metrics mainly rely on some sort of oracle that provides a gold example or a set of possible examples to compare to. I will walk through a characterization of the most widely used metrics such as BLEU, GLEU, NIST 4, ROUGE, TER, and finally ERRANT. I will discuss the underlying ranking algorithms, include error bars and other measures about the sensitivity analysis, and provide recommendations on how to interpret and use them.

## Vertical 2: Transformations of data

This vertical enlightens the safety of bias or pathological conditions. You can imagine in an almost infinite number of ways to transform, combine, or tweak training data in unpredicted ways. I will show a subset of inadvertent combinations that caused unwanted behaviors in fairly traditional NLP pipelines. Among them, I will highlight the failure to propagate special characters, the combination of letters/words between tokens, and, finally, the unwanted associations of definite articles and years for digits occurring in the 1 digitplace of the meter range. I will share how to diagnose the problem, how to detect the specific instances, and how to avoid or control such behaviors in future training stages.

## Vertical 3: Properties of automatic differentiation

This vertical highlights the risks and benefits of using metrics in terms of automatic differentiation. I will show how widely used metrics are layers operating in the training graph, driven by differentiable functions, or other automatic differentiation techniques. I will share some thoughts about when to use them, when to avoid them, the possible side effects, and the potential ways to escape from potential drawbacks of such combination.

## Vertical 4: Diversity in language modeling

This vertical emphasizes the evaluation of expressiveness, diversity, and creativity of a language model's generation. In particular, I will focus on the metrics and measures provided by some NLP pipelines to evaluate different sources of variability during language modeling. Furthermore, I will include the syntactic and phonological properties of tokens and sequences, as well as semantic features that enable a language model to understand or deal with reasoning mechanisms.

Finally, the showcase will point out the likelihood or unlikelihood or unlikely origins of some related NLP pipelines. Pipelines that deal with language and translation modeling are available in https://huggingface.co/transformers/. In addition, pipelines that address comparison or assessment are available in https://huggingface.co/spacy.

# Recommendations

There are several key findings obtained during the project. I share four of them:

- Always diligently inspect the distribution of scores produced by an evaluation metric.
- Do not leave unattended the unexpected data transformations in early stages of data preparation.
- Avoid propagating steps that accelerate automatic differentiation through evaluation metrics whose ranking depends on that differentiation process.
- Encourage and follow diversity of language, not only in natural language understanding and processing but also in design meetings and protocols.

