[91/200] Running: How are fused attention layers implemented?...

🧠 LLM Response:

==========================================

When two or more models are summed or concatenated, it is considered **fusion**. When using convolutions, one
frequent design pattern consists of concatenating the activation tensors from two or more CNNs after processing
the same images. The idea is that the same filters can be learned by several CNN networks, so instead of training several CNNs with the same architecture, it is more efficient to train only one.

Attention models allow for more complex fusion methods. I will give two insights in how attention mechanisms work for multi-attention
models.

Types of Attention Mechanisms
------------------------------

There are three spefic types of attention mechanisms that have different challenges and utility is different as well:

1. Hard attention: Only specific parts of the data are used and all others are completely discarded (only one part is used). These have mainly been used for explaining why a certain prediction has been made. For example, using NER to find out all the regions contributing to recognising a specific term inside an image. Very hard to train, and hardly used now.

2. Soft attention: Using parts of the data probabilistically Just like building a generative model. For example, multiplying the feature maps SWIN/mixformer by a set of points belonging to the external features, which are valuable to sue and forcing them to reconstruct the image according to the union of several experiences. Perform well, used often.

3. Model attention: This is a simple linear combination of outputs in the latent space. It can be implemented with MLPs. We usually need to normalize these attention schemes, since if they aren't producing outputs close to one, all outputs will be essentially biased. Perform very well, and used very often.
   
### Squeeze & Excitation Block

The squeeze and excitation block was first developed for Visual Backbone networks (Wide ResNets, but not for convolutional blocks); it is presented in:

* [Squeeze-and-Excitation Networks (SE Networks)]((https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.01507))

Since it belongs in the spot of Model Attention models, it cannot modify the spatial/temporal features, but just the filter features. From a fitting point of view, the squeeze and excitation block acts similar of batch normalization operations, since it is a set of linear functions;

![Batch](images_content/squeeze.png)

The squeeze operation compresses a high resolution input of tensors with several channels and produces a set of statistics through a 1x1 convolution:

![Batch](images_content/squeeze2.png)

The excitation operation is a couple of operations and modifies the the output by applying a sigmoid function overall. It creates a mask modifying the input and output dimensions and creates a mask by applying the sigmoid function for all values.

![Batch](images_content/squeeze3.png)

These Se blocks are usually very helpful in transfer learning for salience map generation. Several models such as **CIFAR10** may not
be a good knowledge base for object recognition, but using them for a clas of Saliency maps shows strong results and helps transmitting knowledge as a secondary learning objective between models.

### EFNet

For EFNet both feature maps and the attention masks are modified. However, behavior is very similar to Se blocks. They also add dimensionality reduction operations, since they essentially need to squeeze the dimensions of the feature maps. Depending on the architecture, SeNet can be said to define a certain externally influenced Feature discrimination, while EFNet can be used for concentration of feature maps.

An overview of EFNet is presented in:

* [Efficient Channel Attention for Accurate Object Detection in Real-time](http://www.cv-foundation.org/openaccess/content_cvpr_2018/papers/Wang_Efficient_Channel_Attention_for_CVPR_2018_paper.pdf)

In the following paper at CVPR2018, it is shown at a very high level how EFNet applies several attention systems encoding a single attention map based on channel-wise and spatial-wise attention.

![Batch](images_content/efnet.png)

This is the usual block diagram for EFNet. For visualization, please find the picture below:

