**Multi-head Att
[108/200] Running: What are residual connections in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:



Residual connections are essential components in transformer models, which bypass the output of each sub-layer to the input of the next sub-layer. This design ensures that the original source information is preserved and that the model can handle deeper layers more effectively.

In a transformer architecture, these connections are added to each sub-layer, such as the multi-head attention and feed-forward network blocks. By adding the output of a sub-layer to its input, the transformer model can learn more complex patterns and avoid the vanishing gradient problem, which arises in deep neural networks.

Residual connections in transformers enable the model to learn long-term dependencies and significantly improve the model's performance on a wide range of natural language processing tasks.

Here is a visualization of residual connections in transformers:

![Residual Connections in Transformers](https://miro.medium.com/max/1400/1*c4Yrv9F4yvBDe7Xgkx-4Wg@2x.png)

Note that in the image above, "Add & Norm" refers to layer normalization, which is typically applied after each sub-layer in a transformer model.

When training transformers, residual connections allow for more efficient optimization and help to reduce the training time required to achieve good performance. They also provide a regularization effect, reducing overfitting and improving the model's generalization ability.

In summary, residual connections in transformers are essential components that help preserve the original source information, enable the model to learn complex patterns in deeper layers, and improve the model's performance on various natural language processing tasks.
