[112/200] Running: How do checkpoints improve fault tolerance?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Checkpoints are designed to help ensure fault tolerance in a distributed system. They are used to regularly save the states of the involved processes so that, in case of failure, these processes can be restored from the last recorded checkpoint. The system will first roll back to the last checkpoint, and then progress forward using new information starting from this point.

Checkpointing helps improve fault tolerance in two main ways:

1. Reduced recovery time: In the event of a failure, rolling back to the nearest checkpoint is typically much faster than restarting processes from scratch. This is particularly important in systems that cannot afford long periods of downtime during failure recovery.
2. Improved data consistency: Checkpoints are essential to maintain data consistency when failures occur in the distributed system. By periodically saving the state of the system, even if a failure happens, you can roll back to a consistent state and avoid data loss or inconsistency.

In practice, there are various checkpointing techniques, including coordinated and uncoordinated checkpoints, as well as deterministic checkpointing. Regardless of the technique, the primary goal is to improve fault tolerance by reducing recovery time and maintaining data consistency.
