> Mutual information (MI) captures the statistical dependency between two random variables. In neural networks, the MI between the attention head activations in self-attention layers is an effective measure of the redundancy of information in the network. Specifically, we demonstrate how adversarial inversion, a technique for estimating the MI between the activations of two heads against each other in each layer, can act as a simple yet useful metric for analyzing the specialization of individual heads. In particular, if estimators of the MI between the activations of two heads evaluate to zero, we can safely conclude they are orthogonal to each other, and we precisely quantify the deeper meaning of this. To test this, we incrementally prune the top k% most important heads in each layer (where importance is measured using the head pruning metric introduced in Li et al., 2019 and Michel et al., 2019) and empirically evaluate the quality of the remaining subnetwork to obtain a measure of specialization. We find an extremely high and consistent Pearson correlation score of 0.954 measuring the agreement between the normal individual importance score measured over all heads in the layer, and the pruned-sublayer importance score, which is measured by comparing the saliency of k measured heads to all heads in the subsequent pruned layer. Since mutual information and adversarial inversion can be noisy and costly to compute, we then train a new simple classification head wich predicts the saliency of each head in the input token representations of a layer and demonstrate it captures qualitatively similar trends consistent with the importance weights learned from adversarial inversion. This measures head attention distributions for specific tasks (i.e. language translation) and is more agnostic to differences in model architecture, code implementation, and software libraries to facilitate future research via open source contribution

