[140/200] Running: How to ensure reproducible benchmarking?...

🧠 LLM Response:

-------------------------------------------

The default way to benchmark [[kgld]] through [criterion.rs](https://crates.io/crates/criterion) will give you timing information, but it does **not** guarantee that the result is reproducible. Reproducibility in benchmarks needs to be ensured by a separate step, which is described in this chapter.

 kgld's default mode (`-n`) runs in a way that is readily useable with criterion.rs and is good for at-a-glance measurements.
  ```
  cargo bench --bench kgld/fir_512,-n
  ```

However, if you wish to compare two different kernels (e.g. do end-to-end benchmarking, or benchmark two different versions of [[kgld]]), you will wish to use the `-1` measurement to generate a deterministic workload.

1. Generate raw CSV output
	- Insert `-1` measurement.
	- Use collect_csv launcher.
		- For example:
			 ```
			cargo bench --bench kgld/fir_512,-1 -no-default-config -c bench-run-ctts.csv
			```

		- Make sure to use your own name for the output file to not lose any data!
	- Save output to file from `--no-default-config`

2. Read raw CSV output with [[kgld]]'s `--csv` subcommand
	 ```
	  cargo run --manifest-path=bench/Cargo.toml -- -e bench --csv -from=bench-run-ctts.csv
	```
	 - `--manifest-path=bench/Cargo.toml` tells cargo which file to use as the parent
		 - it should point to where your `Cargo.toml` file is located
	 - `-- -e bench` tells cargo which `executable` to use
		 - change the argument to the name of your executable
	 - `--csv` tells the program to read CSV instead of measuring locally
	 - `-from=bench-run-ctts.csv` specifies the file to read from


This will give you the per-kernel timing output.

If you wish to benchmark multiple kernels at once (for example, if you want to benchmark the effect of shared memory on the memory transfers), you can put them into a single the CSV file as follows.

1. generate the CSV file for one kernel.
2. open the file in a text editor and append every kernel that you want to benchmark at the same time.
3. save the changes and run the command from "2." above while specifying the modified `.csv` file.

You can most likely generate the CSV files with other timing utilities, then don't forget to specify the `--hot-cold` to tell `kgld` how long the hot and how long the cold run is. For more information about the `--hot-cold` and formatting of the output file, check `-h` help.
[141/200] Running: What are model shards and when are they used?...

🧠 LLM Response:


A model-shard is a Bag Of Words vector on which a model is trained. The Bag Of Words is nothing more that the histogram of the words appearing in a length of text, thus the Bag Of Words vector can be very high-dimensional, compared to the number of documents stored in a particular index.

Most information retrieval systems will have a large number of bad-of-words vectors, most, if not all the size. The term “model-shard” was introduced to create a distinction between the Bag Of Words vector and the actual model. Usually the model encompasses the Bag Of Words vector and information about all the other vectors (read documents) it was trained on.

Model-shards are used to efficiently train a number of information retrieval models, most of which are linear models.

The reason model-shards are is used, especially in information retrieval applications which typically works with very sparse data is a parallelizing strategy, as multiple model-shards can be trained simultaneously. By training the model-shards simultaneously, the data becomes smaller than the model, and therefore most computations now becomes memory-bound. Part of the reason why many OLAP systems backup to a disk while processing the data is the memory limitation.

Therefore the training process has to be spread memory-bound, making it parallelizable.

In the movie industry, parallelization was used to break up a big scene, so that 500 computers could draw 500 frames, replacing the necessity of using one single huge computer.

Likewise, model-shards breaks the large text corpus into much smaller parts to get rid of the unnecessary overhead of shuffling memory around.

In addition to splitting the data for a one-time training, all learning has to take place globally, as all model-shards must be aggregated after the learning phase. The aggregation of model-shards is a tricky process, this is bi-directional communication between shards. Both sending the data to the same location, and retrieving the accumulated results from the same location.

All this communication can be hard to handle and was Simplified By Google which added binary communications protocols to an existing network. The systems taking advantage of the high-speed networking would be able to transfer data at rates of approximately 10GB/s – thus making the entire model computations become compute-bound.

For lower rates – typical for Big Data setups in a private company, overhead could once again contribute to being the bottleneck, as shards might need to communicate to a central hub where the aggregation takes place. This central hub can also be the cache for the alike models and the alike results can be used for future learning (ex. logistic regression), and thus the boat can sail unhindered by the memory transfer delays.

Today, nobody splits data in model-shards for building a web search engine. Google and most others, use full data for training and subsequent training stages are reserved for determining the best model to use. The main reason is that there are now more sophisticated feature extraction methods, which translates feature sets which can be vectorized and thus determines a Bag Of Words which does not take too much memory.

