Naturally you migrate a (floating point) binary dataset from host A to host B.
Everything is fine. Both hosts use the same operating system (OS), same compiler and the same IEEE (754) floating point format. Great!

However then you realize that you have to transport the same binary dataset
to host C - a platform using a different  processor, or a different OS, a
different compiler or even usage of a different floating point format.
Suddenly it becomes obvious that migrating binary data from one system to
another without carefully considering the floating point encoding,
truncation errors, endianness, literal floating point representations,
type length and character set could be potentially harmful for the data
set integrity. Well in that case you really have to carefully consider
 a safe DATA MIGRATION STRATEGY.

Consider this scenario.

u =  real*8(2.22510223443e-308, 3.40282346638e+38)
  u = 0.0
  u = u + 1.0e-28
  print*,u

