   Here is the output in a single precision floating point encoding.

   H52022203F 00000000 00000000 00000000 3F800000

   0.00000000  2.22044604925e-16  0.00000000  0.00000000
    1.00000011  9209289550781250

And what about a double precision floating point representation?

  0.00000000000000000  4.44089209850063e-16
    1.00000000000000000  0.00000000000000000

So why are there those extra 5 digits in the single precision case (3F800000) and in the double precision (3FF0000000000000)?

Why do we print such bizarre 'precisions' like 4.44...e-16?
If we process the same data on host A (assume the internal floating
point encoding is _both_ single as well as double precision) we
would print:

  1.00000011920928955e-008

  H00000000  3F800000               For single precision

  H00000000  3FF0000000000000       For double precision

What happened?

1. Host A has inherited from the bash/shell the internal conversion
mechanism for input/output floating point values.
Not many user realizes about the 'subtle' difference between
Floating Point Arithmetics as it is translated by the shell
and the actual floating point arithmetic taking place in the processors,
 register and the FPU (Floating Point Unit.)

2. Because on _many_ platforms the conversion of floating point
arithmetics is done via the shell, that means the shell has to define
the format conversions, like the assembly code routines and instructions
 for turning a real value from the fp-register (or a stack in fp mode, etc)
 back to some human-readable text representation.
A conservative implementation (as you see on most UNIX workstations)
does not clip floating point values simply to fit the printout.
Instead it attaches an exponent element to the number.
As you can see in the above case, the literal real value as presented
by the shell has 15 digits while the original mantissa for the
binary encoding were 5 digits (single precision) or a 11 for the
double precision.

