[158/200] Running: What is a quantized model and how is it served?...

🧠 LLM Response:


A quantized model is a sequence model that has been optimized for on-device use cases through quantization. Maps does not do the quantization - this is done offline through ML Kit or other tools.

Quantization reduces the model’s size and also brings some performance advantages, as the quantized model works with 8-bit integer types as opposed to the 32-bit float types of the regular model.  When we serve a model, we wrap it inside a managed package which uses tensorflow lite format. We most commonly serve quantized models hosted on Firebase Cloud Storage, with can be securely accessed from Maps clients. We serve this binary file to your clients via Firebase Cloud Messaging (or to your app's backend server via the Google Cloud Storage XML API).

How long does it take to deliver a model to all clients?

This is easily configurable on our end, but is largely dependent on your desired frequency of updates.  Once the model is served from Firebase Cloud Storage, delivery is very fast via Firebase Cloud Messaging (minutes or even seconds).

Once the clients receive the update, they save the sequential model into the device’s file system and the runtime is ready to process requests.

Can the user update the model themselves, or does it require an app update to receive the new version of the model?

The user device will only receive the model once Maps HQ fires a Cloud Messaging (FCM) update message. If the app version was updated but an FCM message did not come, the user would still be using the old version of the model.

Is the model stored within the app or on the user’s device?

The model is not stored within the app, but saved to the device’s internal or external storage before using it for the first time. If there is no external storage, we can save it into the private storage of the app which will not be accessible to the user.

If the model is not accessible for the user, the model content cannot be edited by the user. Once the model is placed in the storage it will be used by the model runtime provided by Maps.

It is in the user’s discretion on whether they would like to keep the sequential model in the device for future use or delete it once they made their requests. Once the user deletes the model, they will be required to download the model again the next time they made a request.

Does the model ever get processed on the backend, or is it always expected to be processed on the client app?

This can be configured depending on the on-device computing power, internet connection availability, and user’s wish. The ideal sequential model is small enough to be processed on-device, but if there is a need to process the sequence with large quantities of data or save the model results temporarily, the backend service should be used.

For this use case, Maps does not currently provide an off-the-shelf solution. Instead we can focus on optimizing the on-client processing performance and work with the client to configure what content was returned through the API for further processing.

How often are models expected to be updated?

This is dependent on use case, but one example of an update is if a new shape is available for the user’s language, a new language is introduced, or an output becomes more sophisticated.

Do models profiles always need to be recalculated when the model is updated?

No, the model profile is independent of the model. If the model profile was correctly calculated, we can reuse the previously earned model profile. However, as the way that the model is processed may change through new service configurations, the model profile can become invalid and may need an update.

