LensKit’s offline feature extraction (https://github.com/lmthang/LensKit/blob/main/lenskit-web-recommenders/src/main/java/org/grouplens/lenskit/web/recommenders/PredictionAnalysis.java#L242) (tokenizes strings and takes the inverse cosine distance between those tokens to create a 2D vector representation of all the tokens for every user & item) breaks down badly if there are non-English tokens/socially-tagged content. Is there any transform or option to exclude such strings in LensKit? Can one supply a list of terms to exclude as a part of input training/testing data files?
Aside: Where might the best place to start contributing to Lenskit be in terms of fixing the implementation of offline_feature_extraction?

Hi Muhammad,

The Python and R versions of offline\_feature\_extraction have a keyword argument `token_preprocessor` that allows you to specify a function that transforms terms (i.e., strings) into tokens (i.e., lists of strings) that LensKit feeds into the Jaccard similarity computation. An example of this is `lenskit.util.text.stopwords` to exclude stop words in the Jaccard computation.

Java LensKit lacks this implementation so far, unfortunately. It’d be an enhancement, perhaps a pull request for `lenskit.java.algorithms.similarity.VectorSimilarity`, would work. However, this has not been tested by myself and others (including Utkarsh, in our development meeting). Another alternative is to specify a similarity measure acceptable to `VectorToVectorSimilarity` instead, such as `CosineSimilarity` or `CorrelationCosineSimilarity`.

> Can one supply a list of terms to exclude as a part of input training/testing data files?

As a user, including a feature preprocessor could do this for you before invoking offline\_feature\_extraction.py in Python. Java users could do the same. Here’s an example using Stanford’s CoreNLP Java library:

```
Properties props = new Properties();
props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos");
StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);

List<String> stopWords = loadStopsFromFile("english-stopwords.txt");
Pipeline<CoreDocument, CoreDocument>.AnnotatedDocument prepared = pipeline.process(annotateTokenizedText(text));
for (CoreEntity entity : prepared.tokens()) {
  if (!stopWords.contains(entity.lemma())) {
    lemmas.add(entity.lemma());
  }
}

private static List<CoreEntity> annotateTokenizedText(String text) {
  Annotation annotation = new Annotation(text);
  pipeline.annotate(annotation);
  return Collections.unmodifiableList(
    Arrays.stream(annotation.get(CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation.class))
      .filter(object -> object instanceof CoreEntity)
      .map(object -> (CoreEntity) object)
      .collect(Collectors.toList()));
}

private static List<String> loadStopsFromFile(String file) {
  // Load list of stop words from file (one term per line)
  // ...
}
```

See the documentation for text preprocessing.

This might be overkill because this uses CoreNLP to tokenize, split, and tag, however. Since you have the list of stopwords, you could match words in your text using one of those terms and filter the tokens that way. A downside is that you’ll have to take care of positional information (e.g., indices for position `i` in document `a` corresponds to the same index in document `b`).

Keep it simple, and implement a simple regex pattern match for stop words. 😊 In this case, just compile the dictionary into the below pattern.

