🧠 LLM Response:


- Your streaming application has been tasked with scoring models in real-time on huge data streams, perhaps from Kafka.  Each incoming data point or event results in a Spark ML model (e.g. GLM, LR) being updated and the resulting model being served at the endpoint so that responses can be returned in real-time (less than 10ms), along with data coming in.

- The requirements are high concurrency and high performance.

- The incoming data has a dramatic variance in throughput (up to 70K Kafka events per second with a maximum of 70000 maximum Kafka events in a second batch window)

- Not every event will invoke a score ie. 1 in 1000 will score (~700 per second)

- There may be several related models being persisted and served (spanning 15 user segment labels but all using the same attribute set for each user) each will be reloaded as
needed through RESTful APIs (so a RESTful endpoint can trigger the reloading of models in circeise)

- Latency is critical - <10ms per request

- The models are continually updated for specific user segments (and since new user segments could be created at runtime, this could result in an unknown number of models continuing to grow)

- The potential for multi-tenancy streams exists in the future.

You are tasked with coming up with a benchmark for performance.  What would that benchmark look like from the perspectives of (i) speed of model scoring (ii) high throughput of incoming data and (iii) high concurrency?


(i) Speed of model scoring:

* Use Gatling to simulate load (hitting the RESTful API for requesting the model scores) for a large number of concurrent requests. Meanwhile, monitor latency for each requests VIA Spark metrics.


(ii) high throughput of incoming data:

* Use Kafka as the source for the incoming data. Throttle the incoming data to an identified rate of x number of requests per second (ingesting this data into Spark Streaming from Kafka);

* Measure throughput of this data (via Kafka consumer group metrics or by reading the data from the Kafka topic into an external metrics store).


(iii) high concurrency:

* Compute the high concurrency rate by a dual quad core-count compute system as the baseline.
* Measure the number of streams being processed concurrently running against a steady rate of incoming requests over a time period.


To prove that benchmark was met could be achieved  by showing:

* The serverless (stateless) application meets the GLM score requests getting < 10 ms in latency (via Gatling and Spark metric reports)
* The ability to hit 70k Kafka events per second (via monitoring incoming data through consumer metrics, etc.)
* The application could handle 100+ concurrent requests to compute GLM scores before seeing increases in latency/execution time
