[173/200] Running: What causes generation to stall midway?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Generation stop midway is quite common fault with generators in use around the world today, and the question of what causes the fault is a difficult and complex one to answer without more specifics on the signs and symptoms presented by the problem in the particular unit in question or without access to the logs, datasheets and specifics on the affected unit and the fault itself. Here I will try to present a general overview of the most common causes of midway stall fault in generator units with a combustion engine.

Before going into the specifics of the fault, it is important to understand that the generator is a unit powered by a combustion engine, which draws in fuel, which is supplied under pressure from a fuel pump. The engine then mixes the fuel with air from the atmosphere in exactly right proportions and ignites this fuel-air mixture via the spark plug. A proper explosion ensues, driving the combustion engine pistons, which in turn drive the crankshaft, which is connected to the alternator of the generator unit. Then the alternator transforms the mechanical power into alternating current electrical power, which is supplied to the load through the output cables, depending on the configuration of the generator unit.

There is a number of reasons why generation can stall midway. I will try to cover the most common reasons below in order of frequency in real-life scenarios. It must be noted that the continuation of the list does not imply that any particular reason is more probable than the others, but rather this list represents the general overall trend in experience in many countries around the world.

Electrical load is too high for generator capacity

Generator units are sized to match the maximum electrical power draw that will ever be placed on the generators through the output cables is designed to handle. Whenever the existing electrical load exceeds the designed maximum electrical rating of the generator or the output cables, there is a serious problem and the generator unit will stall midway until the electrical load is reduced.

This situation is especially dangerous if the electrical load exceeds the design maximum rating of the alternator, generator output voltage will permanently deteriorate, often leading to permanent damage to the alternator. Output electrical current will be limited as well, often triggering an overcurrent protection which might cause limited operation or stops rotation of engine. Many combustion engine generators are equipped with special built-in protection against electrical overload, but the protection functions only during the start up phase of the engine, so after that it is the responsibility of the users to monitor the electrical loads and reduce them accordingly before continuing operation of the generator.

Fuel level or supply is insufficient

Fuel is always required by the generator unit to keep running. fuel has to be present in the tank so that the fuel pump can provide the essential fuel under pressure. Generators with low fuel levels will stall midway whenever the fuel pump fails to provide enough fuel for combustion. When this happens, it is important to refuel the generator unit as soon as possible to prevent occurrences of engine stall while in use.

This stall is typically not dangerous for the rig itself but could be for the engine, as the fuel pump can start heating up, as well as some parts of operation engine, because of fuel inlet restriction or bad fuel flow sensor.

Incorrect fuel-air mixture due to spark plug crack or dirty spark plug

Generator operate the engine with spark ignition where air- fuel mixture is ignited by electrical spark generated at high voltage. Maintenance of spark plugs is really critical for every ignition engine. any missing spark or misfiring will lead to engine vibration, and decreasing of combustion efficiency, which first will lead to smaller output power and then blockage to keep rotation at planned rpm. Incorrect output or power will lead to engine stall or its blocking for further operations.

If the spark plug is cracked or dirty, the generator waves cannot ignite the air-fuel mixture inside the combustion cylinder properly, leading to power imbalances and thus engine stall midway. Proper, regular maintenance of spark plugs is always critical to prevent engine problems such as this.

Solution is proper cleaning of spark plug at planned maintenance intervals, or if spark plug is cracked then it will have to be replaced.

Inanition of battery

Generator units are electrically-powered devices and run off a battery powered by a 12V direct current source. similar to most other motorized equipment with electricity source, generator must have a battery or starter motor or starter pilot. With no proper battery voltage, generator cannot start its engine automatically, also even if it is running the electronic controls will not work
