[178/200] Running: What are temperature vs randomness in sampling?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 It turns out that these 2 questions are very well related! Let me show you why!

## Importance sampling

Imagine we have the following distribution we wish to sample:

A 1 dimensional distribution. X is sampled from \( [-1,1] \)

To sample from this distribution, we can use the inverse transform method. Here we take the cumulative distribution and then use the inverse CDF to map our values evenly across the input distribution. It’s in fact just the inverse quantile function.

Let’s use the inverse transform method with some noise to get something that explores our function.

We can see that the area under the density is the same, and therefore this exploration is unbiased.

What if we used importance sampling? We could sample from an easier to sample, normalized distribution.

And and weight our approximated draws by the area under the easier distribution that contributed to our sample.

We can even use monte carlo integration to understand how many draws would be required to get an area of 0.938 using the importance sampling approach.

```
import tqdm

total_weight = 0

for _ in tqdm.tqdm(range(100_000_000)):
    sample = np.random.uniform(-1, 1, 32)

    weight = n * np.expand_dims(p(sample), -1)
    total_weight += weight.sum()

print(total_weight * (sample_interval))
0.9375345783254968
```

We can use these techniques to sample in higher dims. Such as sampling from a multi dimensional gaussian.

It’s nice to have an unbiased estimator, but we are wasting evaluations when we sample far from a function, especially in high dims when importance distributions are harder to come by!

## Bias

Imagine we did in fact know the true distribution and wanted to use this to sample. We can use this density to bias our exploration! The resulting estimator is no longer unbiased!

We now have something which will never stray away from the density (we can’t sample where the density is zero). This will highly concentrate our samples near the density peak! We can compute the distribution, resulting in:

So our sampling is no longer unbiased. This may be fine depending on our use case! Extremely good at exploring the density, extremely poor at exploring the areas where the density is 0!

How bad is the bias if we oversample by $\sigma=5$, the area of the peak is amplified $\sigma^2 = 25 \times$ over the surrounding areas. This is a large bias!

## Randomness

First lets compute a better estimator!

The slope doesn’t look great, but is zero when the hypothesis is true.

Our estimator works great! But it kinda reminds we of early creep in reinforcement learning. It is focussed highly on the areas where the function is high and pays very little weight to the area where the function is low. We can use randomness to address this!

Rather than evaluating in 1 location at a time, we can randomly sample a set of locations! The number of samples must be equal to or less than the amount of bias!

Given this increased randomness our estimator is more robust against over bias and the estimator is now backed by randomness (more than bias). Intuitively causing a lock in to an exact area.

Intuitively, as the STD of the bias increases, the randomness should decrease. This is also true in the other direction.

The optimal confidence has been solved elsewhere. You can refer to the KL uncertainty bonus paper if you are interested.

## Source

I wrote! I hope I conveyed the point and that this helped you somehow! This will also be taught in a how to theoretically use ensembles too! Which I am planning to write soon!

If you wish to cite this work, please consider citing me as:

```
@misc{lessel2020sampling,
    title={Sampling by Temperature vs. Randomness},
    author={J\_Lessel},
    howpublished={\url{https://jlessel.github.io}},
    year={2020}
}
```

