==================================================

To understand what could possibly go wrong in a vLLM serving process, we need to first understand how vLLM operates and how it is integrated with the serving stack. Here is a quick refresh:

* `vLLM <viperlang/serve.md#vllm>`__ (also called viper serving) is a language model server that wraps the model training process into a production-ready microservice.
* The serving stack includes `viper-api <viperlang/serve.md#viper-api>`__, `viper chat <viperlang/serve.md#viperchat>`__, `viper question and answer <viperlang/serve.md#viperqa>`__, and `viper keyword <viperlang/serve.md#viperkws>`__ for different use cases, each of which talks to vLLM in JSON over HTTP.
* `viper-engine <viperlang/serve.md#viperengine>`__ powers the `vLLM <viperlang/serve.md#vllm>`__ and the serving stack with natural language processing functions.
* `viperlib <viperlang/serve.md#viperlib>`__ contains other utility functions for the whole serving stack, such as multi-task parallelization, persistence, and much more.
* vLLM also makes use of the `LLMService API <viperlang/llm.md#llmservice>`__, which is a set of HTTP API functions that can be used to modify an existing language model, create a new model, or even train a new one.

Based on the architecture above, the following are the main points where trouble could arise:

* `LLMService API <viperlang/llm.md#llmservice>`__ provides the underlying engine for the serving stack, and if it fails or is not functional, the whole serving stack will fail.
* `viperlib <viperlang/serve.md#viperlib>`__ is a companion library for both the serving stack and vLLM, so any issues with it would also affect the serving stack/vLLM.
* The vLLM daemon could potentially fail if the model it is using has been rolled back (i.e., the model is no longer available) or has been corrupted for any reason.
* The vLLM daemon could potentially fail if there are concurrency issues, such as multiple processes trying to write to the same model at the same time.
* The vLLM daemon could potentially fail if the underlying keystore either isn't available, is changed, or becomes inconsistent between different applications.
* The serving stack could fail if the underlying vLLM daemon is not available, or if there isn't one present in the system.
* The serving stack could potentially fail if the model that is being used isn't available or is corrupted.
* The serving stack could potentially fail if there are concurrency issues related to the model, the endpoint, or the underlying keystore, similar to vLLM.
* The serving stack could potentially fail if a particular service the stack relies on goes down, or if there is an issue with network connectivity between the services in question.

Diagnosing and Solving Issues
============================

When the vLLM or any of the services in the serving stack fail, it is imperative to have a quick diagnostic process to understand the issue and either roll back to a previous version, or to fix any bugs that have been found.

Here are the first steps to take when diagnosing a problem with the serving stack or vLLM:

* Check the health status of each service that is part of the serving stack through the `Health API function <https://gitlab.com/viperlang/viperlang/blob/5668adc7fcbe1a5e044f5c27bb6a20a5c0d5d76c/llmservice/api/api.py#L14>`__.
* Check the master build status of the project. This can be found on `viperlang's build page on Gitlab <here>`__.
* If there is a major issue that needs to be resolved, check for an incident report or an issue report related to the problem. Such incidents will be posted to the `discord server <https://discord.gg/kXMU8aF>`__ as well Sentry. Otherwise, consider opening a new
